    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d393">

                <pb facs="0442" n="358" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp02/pg358"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. No. 837.2222/5</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Minister in
                        Cuba</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gonzales</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-07-23T18:00:00-04:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 23, 1918, 6 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your July 13, 1 p.m. It is deemed highly advisable that the legislation similar
                    to American Trading with Enemy Act should contain in it provisions: (1) that the
                    transfer of physical assets of German properties to non-enemies be free from all
                    enemy interests of any character and from all obligations or contracts to
                    enemies: (2) that the proceeds of the sale of these physical assets of the
                    German properties be held until the termination of the war, at which time their
                    ultimate disposition shall then be determined upon.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Polk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
